<http://data.silknow.org/object/55c5be5b-7c2e-367f-8cac-0a81937d0172/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "This textile cover is called a 'fukusa'. Traditionally in Japan, gifts were placed in a box on a tray, over which a fukusa was draped. The choice of a fukusa appropriate to the occasion was an important part of the gift-giving ritual.  This fukusa is woven with a design of cranes which are a symbol og longevity in Japan."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/55c5be5b-7c2e-367f-8cac-0a81937d0172> .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/55c5be5b-7c2e-367f-8cac-0a81937d0172>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Gift cover (fukusa) of tapestry woven silk, Japan (Edo), second half of 19th century"@en , "Gift cover (fukusa) of tapestry woven silk depicting cranes. Silk with a navy blue ground on which are four cranes woven with grey, white and blue feathers, and picked out with gold thread. There is a plain strip of white at the top and bottom of the panel. Sewn to one corner is a paper tab with an inscription in Japanese."@en , "This textile cover is called a 'fukusa'. Traditionally in Japan, gifts were placed in a box on a tray, over which a fukusa was draped. The choice of a fukusa appropriate to the occasion was an important part of the gift-giving ritual.  This fukusa is woven with a design of cranes which are a symbol og longevity in Japan."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/0c292f11-234c-32ca-83a5-47dba4abf3c8> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Gift cover (fukusa) of tapestry woven silk, Japan (Edo), second half of 19th century"@en , "Gift cover (fukusa) of tapestry woven silk depicting cranes. Silk with a navy blue ground on which are four cranes woven with grey, white and blue feathers, and picked out with gold thread. There is a plain strip of white at the top and bottom of the panel. Sewn to one corner is a paper tab with an inscription in Japanese."@en , "This textile cover is called a 'fukusa'. Traditionally in Japan, gifts were placed in a box on a tray, over which a fukusa was draped. The choice of a fukusa appropriate to the occasion was an important part of the gift-giving ritual.  This fukusa is woven with a design of cranes which are a symbol og longevity in Japan."@en ;
